   <item>
    <widget class="SafeTextEdit" name="helpText">
     <property name="accessibleName">
      <string>Contact Manager Help</string>
     </property>
     <property name="tabChangesFocus">
      <bool>true</bool>
     </property>
     <property name="plainText">
      <string>&gt;&gt; 1. COMMON SHORTCUTS
  Control-F - Find in Current Page (F3 - Find Next)
  Control-G - Find Global (all Contacts)
  Control-Shift-G - Advanced Global Find
  Control-J - Open Journal for Contact in Easy Notepad
  Control-L - Open To-Do List for Contact
  Control-Shift-L - Open My To-Do List
  Control-M - Send Email to Contact
  Control-Shift-M - Send SMS to Contact
  Control-N - Create New Appointment for Contact
  Control-Shift-N - Create New Appointment for Me
  Control-I - Insert Appointment After the Selected One
  Control-Shift-I - Insert Appointment for Me After the Selected One
  Control-U - Update the Selected Appointment
  Control-R - Remove the Selected Appointment
  Control-O - Open / Create Contact
  Control-Shift-O - Open My Contact
  Control-S - Save Current Contact
  Control-T - Goto Today (in Calendar)
  Control-D - Goto Date (in Calendar)
  F1 - This Manual
  F3 - Find Next
  F9 - Google Sync
  Alt-O - Goto Overview Tab
  Alt-C - Goto Calendar Tab
  Alt-L - Goto ToDo List Tab
  Alt-D - Goto Contact Details Edit Tab (if Enabled with Alt-E, D)
  Alt-Y - Goto History Tab

&gt;&gt; 2.  LAYOUT
  Contact Manager is an application with a menu across the top, and below it, 
  a set of tabs, each containing different information.
  You can access the menus, the tabs, and some shortcuts within the tabs with
  the ALT key.  You will also find that the shortcuts mentioned in the previous 
  section can also be found on the menus.  Note that not all menu options and 
  tabs are available in all scenarios (for example, if no contact is open, the 
  Contact Details tab is disabled).

&gt;&gt; 2.1. MENUS AND TABS
  Menus: File (Alt-F); Search (Alt-S); Goto (Alt-G); Edit (Alt-E); 
         Tasks (Alt-T); Help (Alt-H)
  Tabs:  Overview (Alt-O); Calendar (Alt-C); To-Do List (Alt-L); ...
         Contact Details (Alt-D); History (Alt-Y)

&gt;&gt; 3. OPENING A CONTACT
  There are three different ways to open a contact: File/Open, Searching, and 
  Selecting an Appointment:

&gt;&gt; 3.1. FILE/OPEN
  Opening an existing Contact can be done with the open menu: Select Alt-F,O 
  or Control-O.  Navigate the Open Menu with the up/down arrow keys.  You can 
  also press the first letter of a surname to quick-skip.

&gt;&gt; 3.2. GLOBAL SEARCH
  You can also use the Global Find to search for and open contacts: Select 
  Alt-S,G, or Control-G.  Enter part of the name (this is not case sensitive, 
  and accented characters are not necessary), and press Enter. You will then 
  be presented with a list of matching records - use the up/down arrows to 
  navigate the list, and press Enter to open the contact.

&gt;&gt; 3.3. APPOINTMENTS
  Finally you can also open a contact by selecting a calendar entry for that
  contact, and pressing Enter.

&gt;&gt; 4. CREATING AND EDITING CONTACTS

&gt;&gt; 4.1. OPENING / EDITING
  To Create a New Contact, Select File/Open (Control-O), and navigate to the 
  top of the list, or press minus to jump straight to the '-- create contact --' 
  entry.  Press Enter, and you will be taken to a blank Contact Details Tab.
  If you open an existing contact, the Contact Details Tab is disabled for 
  safety - you can enable editing with Alt-E,D (Edit Details), which will enable 
  the tab, and move the cursor to the first field.

&gt;&gt; 4.2. NAVIGATING THE EDIT FORM
  The Edit form is a set of information entry boxes.  The form is laid out this
  way to force details to be entered in a prescribed format - this helps when
  synchronising with email applications, mobile phones, and with automated 
  functions such as email and text message reminders.
  The tab key can be used to switch between the entry boxes, in addtion, the 
  form is organised into sections - the cursor can be moved from section to 
  section with Alt-navigation keys: Alt-N, Alt-M, Alt-A, Alt-P

&gt;&gt; 4.2.1. NAMES (Alt-N)
  Name:         This is the first name (or names of the contact)
  Surname:       This is the contact's surname
  Organisation: This is the contact's business name
  Type: This is a series of tick boxes - select one or more for the contact.
        For contacts that are not currently needed, they can be hidden, 
        rather than deleted.  These contacts won't sync with mobile
        phones, or appear in the open menu, but they will appear 
        (in brackets) at the bottom of any Global Search (Control-G).

&gt;&gt; 4.2.2. EMAIL (Alt-M)
  There are four email addresses.  Each has a title, such as Home, Work or Other, and
  Google sends the title with the address.  Press F2 in an address box to change its
  title.  A title can also be typed after the address, as in name@domain.com (Work).
  A title can use up to 20 characters, and cannot contain a colon, slash or backslash.
  An address must look like name@domain.com.  The form is checked when it is saved,
  and a badly formatted address is shown with the cursor in its box, so nothing is
  saved until it is right.
  Web Site:     The contact's web site

&gt;&gt; 4.2.3. ADDRESS (Alt-A)
  It is recommended that the address is formatted neatly, as it would appear on 
  an envelope, without commas or full-stops at the end of each line.
  Address:      The contact's first address
  2nd Address:  The contact's second address
    
&gt;&gt; 4.2.4. PHONE (Alt-P)
  Phone numbers are stored in the +nn international format, and are formatted when the
  form is saved.  Each number has a title, such as Home, Work, Mobile or Other.  Press F2
  in a number box to change its title.  A title can also be typed after the number, as
  in 01234 567890 (Home).  A title follows the same rules as an email title.
  A number may use digits, spaces, brackets, hyphens and dots, with an optional + at the
  start.
  SIP / VOIP:   A SIP address for VOIP or Skype, such as sip:name@domain

&gt;&gt; 4.2.5. BIRTHDAY, COMMENTS AND OPTIONS
  Birthday:     This is the contact's birthday.  You can edit it with the
                up/down arrow keys, or type the date in numbers.  Note that 
                you don't need to enter the year, but if you do, it is the year
                expressed as 4 digits.  If you want to remove the year, press
                minus.  e.g. Entering 15091969 will result in the field being 
                set to 15/Sep/1969.  Pressing minus when editing the year will 
                result in 15/Sep/----.
  Comment:      This is a free-text comment field, that is saved in the contact 
                record, and is accessible through any synchronised mobile phone.
                This is for any additional information, such as RIBs.  Note: Do
                not enter any passwords in this field.
  Confirm SMS:  Enables SMS being sent for appointment reminders.
  Confirm Email:Enables Emails being sent for appointment reminders.

&gt;&gt; 5. MANAGING APPOINTMENTS
  By default, Contact Manager shows the Calendar Tab (Alt-C).  Appointments are
  listed in date and time order, from 90 days before today to 730 days after it
  (windowbefore and windowafter in the ini file).  A repeating appointment is
  listed for each of its dates.  A deleted appointment is listed only when
  File > Show > Deleted Appointments is ticked, and then it is in braces.

  Moving around the list (the calendar list has the focus):
    Up and Down arrows  Move to the previous or next entry
    T or Control-T      Go to today: the first entry from now on
    D or Control-D      Go to a date (a date box, as below)
    0 to 9              Start typing a date (see below)
    W                   Go a week on from today.  Each W pressed after it goes a
                        week further on: WW is two weeks on, WWW three, and so on,
                        with no limit.  T or Control-T starts again from today
    N or Right arrow    Go to the next entry for the open contact.  After the last
                        one, it wraps round to the first
    P or Left arrow     Go to the previous entry for the open contact
    Space               Show only the open contact's entries, or all of them again
    Enter               Open the contact for the selected entry (see section 3.3)

  With no contact open, Space, N, P and the arrows beep, and nothing changes.
  Moving to a date, or pressing Control-T or W, shows all the entries again if
  Space had shown only one contact's.  Each move puts the entry about a quarter
  of the way down the list, so the entries coming up after it are seen.

  Typing a date
  Press 0, 1, 2 or 3 in the calendar list, and a box opens with that digit in
  it, and the cursor at the end.  Type the rest of the date, and press Enter.
  The list goes to the first entry on that date, or to the nearest entry before
  it if there is none.  Escape, or Enter on an empty box, closes the box and
  leaves the list as it was.  Text that is not a date closes the box and beeps.

  A date can be typed in numbers, day first:
    2/12          2 December, the next one on or after today
    2-12-26       2 December 2026
    2/12/2026     2 December 2026
    2/12/26       2 December 2026
  or with the name of the month, in full or in its first three letters or more:
    12 oct 2026   12 October 2026
    1 sep         1 September, the next one on or after today
    1aug28        1 August 2028
  If the year is left out, the next date with that day and month, on or after
  today, is used.  A year of two digits takes the first two digits of this year.
  Day names (mon, tue and so on) are not accepted.

  Creating and changing appointments
    Control-N           New appointment for the open contact
    Control-Shift-N     New appointment for me
    Control-I           Insert an appointment after the selected one
    Control-Shift-I     Insert an appointment for me after the selected one
    Control-U           Update the selected appointment
    Control-R           Remove the selected appointment
  Contact Manager checks for clashes before an appointment is saved, and offers
  you the chance to change it.  At startup it also lists any appointments that
  overlap, but changes nothing.

&gt;&gt; 5.1. THE APPOINTMENT FORM 
  The form is laid out with four fields:
  For:          The contact the appointment is for
  Summary:      A one-line of the appointment
  From:         The start date /time of the appointment, and the duration
  To:           The end date / time of the appointment
  Description:  A multi-line description of the appointment
  The From and To edits work in much the same way as the Birthday field in the
  Contact Details - you can navigate with the arrow keys, or type in dates
  (and times) numerically.  The duration (in brackets) can be changed with
  the up/down arrow keys - if you want a duration other than those offered,
  you wll have to modify the To field.  The From and To fields are linked, and
  adjusting one affects the other - normally, you will only need to edit the
  From field.
  When you are happy with the appointment, you can press enter to save, or
  escape to cancel. Contact manager will check for clashes before saving, 
  and offer you the opportunity to change / update.

&gt;&gt; 6. TOOLS

&gt;&gt; 6.1. TRANSACTIONS
  When a contact is opened, transaction entries can be made in the log.  Press
  Control+ to add income from the contact, and Control- to add an expense to 
  the contact.
  You can get a transaction report for the year with Alt-T,R.

&gt;&gt; 6.2. EMAILING
  You can launch your favorite mail program with an email readied for the current
  contact with Control-M

&gt;&gt; 6.3. AUTOMATIC MESSAGES
  ContactManager emails or texts a contact by itself when an appointment is made,
  changed or cancelled, and on the day of the appointment.  The contact must have
  Email or Text ticked, with an address or a mobile number, to be messaged.  A
  deleted or hidden contact is not messaged.

  Messages are only sent between earliestsend and latestsend (07:00 and 22:00 by
  default, local time).  A new or changed appointment waits waitbeforesendmins (5
  minutes) after its last change, so that mistakes can be corrected first.

  Which message, and when:
    Confirmation  When a new appointment is made.  Sent by email if Email is ticked,
                  otherwise by text.  If both are ticked, it is sent by email.
    Update        When an appointment is changed, once the wait has passed.  Sent
                  on the same channel.
    Reminder      remindhoursbefore hours before the appointment (24 by default).
                  Not sent on the day of the appointment, as the on-the-day
                  messages take its place.
    Confirm only  An appointment made or changed less than confirmonlyhours before it
                  starts (36 by default) gets its confirmation or update only.  That
                  message takes the place of the reminder.
    Cancellation  When an appointment is deleted, straight away, on the same channel
                  (email if both are ticked).
    On the day    From earliestsend on the day of the appointment, each channel the
                  contact has is sent once: an email if Email is ticked, then a text
                  if Text is ticked.  A confirmation or update sent by email that day
                  gives that day's email; one sent by text gives that day's text.
                  The other channel is still sent.
    Made late     An appointment made the day before, after the window had closed,
                  is confirmed when the window opens on the day.  Its email is the
                  confirmation.  Its text is the on-the-day text, with no
                  confirmation text.

  Missed messages: if ContactManager is not running, or a message is held, the
  message is sent when it can be, showing the current details.  A reminder is not
  sent once the appointment is on that day.

  Changing channels: if Email or Text is changed after an appointment has been
  messaged, only the messages still pending on the new channel are sent.  Nothing
  already sent is sent again.

  Limits: a contact is sent no more than limitperday messages (10) in any 24 hours,
  and limitperhour (5) in any hour.  On-the-day messages count.  Messages over a
  limit wait until it allows them.

  Examples.  The times are the defaults.  Each example shows what is sent to a
  contact with Email only, Text only, or both ticked.

  1. Made at 10:00 three days before, for 15:00 on the day.
     Email only: confirmation email at 10:05.  Reminder email at 15:00 the day
                 before.  On the day, from 07:00, an email.
     Text only:  confirmation text at 10:05.  Reminder text at 15:00 the day
                 before.  On the day, from 07:00, a text.
     Both:       confirmation email at 10:05.  Reminder email at 15:00 the day
                 before.  On the day, from 07:00, an email, then a text.

  2. As 1, but moved to 16:00 at 10:00 two days before.
     Update at 10:05 two days before, on the same channel.  Reminder at 16:00 the
     day before.  On the day, as in 1.

  3. Made at 15:00 the day before, for 16:00 the next day.
     Email only: confirmation email at 15:05, which also serves as the reminder.
                 On the day, from 07:00, an email.
     Text only:  confirmation text at 15:05, which also serves as the reminder.
                 On the day, from 07:00, a text.
     Both:       confirmation email at 15:05, which also serves as the reminder.
                 On the day, from 07:00, an email, then a text.

  4. Made at 08:00 on the day, for 15:00.
     Email only: confirmation email at 08:05, which also serves as the reminder.
     Text only:  confirmation text at 08:05, which also serves as the reminder.
     Both:       confirmation email at 08:05, which also serves as the reminder.
                 Then, straight after, a text, as the confirmation was an email.

  5. Made at 08:00 on the day, for 15:00, and cancelled at 14:00.
     Confirmation at 08:05.  Cancellation at 14:00, straight away, on the same
     channel (email if both are ticked).  Nothing else is sent.

  6. Made at 08:00 on the day, for 15:00, and moved to 15:00 the next day at 14:00.
     Confirmation at 08:05.  Update at 14:05.  No reminder at 15:00 on the first day,
     as the update is less than 36 hours before the new time.  On the new day, from
     07:00: Email only, an email; Text only, a text; Both, an email, then a text.

  7. Made at 23:30 the day before, for 12:00 on the day.
     Nothing is sent at 23:30, as the window is closed.  At 07:00 on the day:
     Email only: confirmation email, which also gives the day's email.
     Text only:  an on-the-day text.  There is no confirmation text.
     Both:       confirmation email at 07:00, then, straight after, a text.

  8. Made at 10:00 three days before, for 15:00 on the day.  ContactManager is not
     running from 11:00 three days before until 06:00 on the day.
     Confirmation at 10:05, before it stopped.  The reminder for 15:00 the day before
     is not sent, as it was missed, and by the time ContactManager runs again the
     appointment is on that day.  On the day, from 07:00: Email only, an email;
     Text only, a text; Both, an email, then a text.

  Two copies of ContactManager on the same data folder do not both send messages.
  Only the copy that holds the lock in the data folder sends them.

&gt;&gt; 6.4. THE FILE MENU: SHOW, TIDY RECORDS AND LOGS
  File > Show > Deleted Appointments, Deleted Contacts and Hidden Contacts each
  add those entries to the lists, when they are ticked.  The ticks are not kept,
  so they are off each time Contact Manager starts.
  File > Tidy Records saves any edit on the screen first, then tidies the history
  and contact records.  Its progress window shows a summary of what was changed.
  File > Show > Logs opens the log window.  It is not modal, so you can carry on
  using Contact Manager while it is open, and it shows new entries as they happen.

&gt;&gt; 7. SYNCHRONISING

&gt;&gt; 7.1. SYNCHRONISING AND CONFLICT MANAGEMENT
   Pressing F9 causes the contact details and appointments to be synchronised 
   with Google.  Any phones or email packages that also synchronise with
   he same account will be updated.
   Contact Manager can be set to be the master, in cases of conflict - this is
   the recommended setting, and means that any erroneous modifications made using
   other devices will be repaired through a contact manager synchronisaiton.
   This does mean that edits made on other devices will be lost, however, new
   contacts and appointments made on remote devices will automatically be added.
   Alternatively, Google can be set to be the master. Any modifications made are 
   recorded in the History for the appropriate contact, and can be found on the 
   History tab (Alt-Y).

   Appointments are downloaded from Google from one year back, up to the end of
   the appointment window set in File/Setup.  A repeating appointment is downloaded
   with its first occurrence, so if that is more than a year old it is not
   downloaded, and its repeats are not shown.  Synchronise at least once a year:
   anyone who does not is not really using Google sync.

&gt;&gt; 8. CONFIGURATION

   Configuration is stored ContactManager.ini file, which is located in either:
    $HOME/ContactManager
    $HOME/TrumptonApps
    Same Directory as ContactManager.exe

   Additional Configuration is stored in the registry, and this is managed with File/Settings

   The ini file is split into a number of sections:
    general - General configuration
    database - Location of the database files
    journal - Path to the journal files, and EasyNotepad command
    google - Google synchronisation options
    phone - Local and country dialling codes
    sms - SMS agent (Voodoo or Esendex), its username and API key, the sender, and debug files. The Settings page also has the API key expiry date (day/month/year, for example 25/01/2035, optional). A warning appears at startup from 30 days before it.
    email - Email configuration for sending reminder messages (server, username, sender, debug files), plus launching email application
    reminders - When messages are sent: the reminder (remindhoursbefore, default 24), the confirm-only window (confirmonlyhours, default remindhoursbefore x 1.5: an appointment made or changed this close to its start gets its confirmation or update only), the sending times (earliestsend and latestsend, local time), the hold before a message is sent after a change (waitbeforesendmins, default 5, 0 allowed), the most messages to one contact in any 24 hours (limitperday, default 10) and in any hour (limitperhour, default 5), counted over the last 24 hours and the last hour, so they hold across a restart, and whether an .ics is attached to emails (attachics, default no).  With Google sync, the sent state of each appointment with a contact is kept on its Google event, so copies of ContactManager on the same calendar do not send the same message twice
    message - Email and SMS message files. The first line is the sender: the sender key in sms-reminder or email-reminder overrides it when set.

   The ini file supports a number of variables
    $HOME - The user's home directory
    $DIRAPPLICATION - Path to the application directory
    $INI - Path to the folder where the INI file was loaded from
    $CONFIG - $HOME/TrumptonApps
    $TO - To email address (when sending an email - Control-M)
    $NAME - Recipient email name (when sending an email - Control-M)


&gt;&gt; 8.1. EXAMPLE
 
[general]
codec=UTF-8

[database]
path=&quot;$CONFIG/ContactManager&quot;

[journal]
path=&quot;$CONFIG/EasyNotepad/Massage Clients&quot;
easynotepadexe=&quot;$DIRAPPLICATION/EasyNotepad.exe&quot;

[google]
priority=ask
debug=yes

[phone]
localcode=01480
countrycode=0044

[sms-reminder]
agent=voodoo
username=
sender=
debug=no

[email-reminder]
server=smtp.gmail.com
username=username@gmail.com
port=465
sender=
debug=no

[reminders]
remindhoursbefore=24
confirmonlyhours=36
earliestsend=07:00
latestsend=22:00
waitbeforesendmins=5
limitperday=10
limitperhour=5
attachics=no

[email]
command=&quot;\&quot;C:\\Program Files (x86)\\Mozilla Thunderbird\\thunderbird.exe\&quot; -compose \&quot;subject='Message to $NAME',to='$TO'\&quot; &quot;

[message]
emailconfirmation=&quot;$INI/emailconfirmation.msg&quot;
emailupdate=&quot;$INI/emailupdate.msg&quot;
emaildelete=&quot;$INI/emaildelete.msg&quot;
emailreminder=&quot;$INI/emailreminder.msg&quot;
smsconfirmation=&quot;$INI/emailconfirmation.msg&quot;
smsupdate=&quot;$INI/smsupdate.msg&quot;
smsdelete=&quot;$INI/smsdelete.msg&quot;
smsreminder=&quot;$INI/smsreminder.msg&quot;

</string>
     </property>
     <property name="textInteractionFlags">
      <set>Qt::TextSelectableByKeyboard|Qt::TextSelectableByMouse</set>
     </property>
    </widget>
   </item>
  </layout>
 </widget>
